    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 53,
      "text": "\nRole of Toxins in Disease Development in Plants\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 116,
      "text": "Dr. Amit Chauhan\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Plant Pathology\nUdai Pratap College (Autonomous), Varanasi-221002"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 364,
      "text": "Toxins as low molecular weight, non-enzymatic microbial products which are toxic to higher plants. \nIn Plant pathology, the term toxin is used for a product of the pathogen, its host or pathogen-host interaction. \nIt occurs at very low concentration and directly acts on living host protoplasm \nto influence the course of disease development or symptom expression."
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 206,
      "text": "The idea of toxins play an important role in disease development in plants was enunciated by Anton De Bary in 1886. \nGeaumann (1954) believed that “microorganism are pathogenic only if they are toxigenic.”\n"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 529,
      "text": "Toxins, interact with the cell membrane or the organelles (mitochondria or chloroplast) and alter their permeability. \nIt causes loss of water and electrolytes and also unrestricted entry of substances including toxins. \nCellular transport system, especially, H+ / K+ exchange at the cell membrane is affected. \nToxin disturb salt balance of host cell, so that it may cause-\n\tincrease in respiration rate.\n   lacking in photosynthesis area\n\tmalfunctioning of enzyme system\n\tdeath of host cells\n\tHydrolysis of protoplasmic protein"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 144,
      "text": "Classification of Toxins: \nAccording to Wheeler and Luke (1963) based on the source of origin, toxins were classified into three broad classes. "
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 335,
      "text": "Phytotoxins: Any compound produced by a microorganism which is toxic to plants is a phytotoxin. These are toxic to both, host as well as non-host plants (non-specific in action), incite few or none of the symptoms that are incited by the pathogens. \nFor example, Alternaric acid produced by Alternaria solani, Tentoxin and Oxalic acid."
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 10,
      "text": "Vivotoxins"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 11,
      "text": "Pathotoxins"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 12,
      "text": "Phytotoxins "
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 65,
      "text": "In recent classification, toxins are divided into two categories:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 171,
      "text": "1. Host non-specific Toxins: They affect many unrelated plant species in addition to the main host of the pathogen producing toxin. It includes Phytotoxins and Vivotoxins."
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 101,
      "text": "2. Host specific Toxins: They affect only the specific host of the pathogen. It includes Pathotoxins."
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 20,
      "text": "Host-Specific Toxins"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 24,
      "text": "Host Non-Specific Toxins"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 282,
      "text": "2. Vivotoxins: Defined as substance produced by the pathogen in the infected host which is involved in the production of disease but not initial incident of the disease itself. Vivotoxins may also induce only a part of disease symptoms. \n\nFor example, Fusaric acid and Pyricularin.\n"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 281,
      "text": "3. Pathotoxins: They are defined as host specific toxins which induce all the typical symptoms of the disease that is caused by the producer pathogen at reasonable concentration. These toxins may be produced by the pathogen, host or interaction between them. \nFor example, Victorin"
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "TEXTBOX",
      "characters": 1,
      "text": "￼"
    },
    {
      "kind": "NOTE",
      "characters": 0,
      "text": ""
    },
    {
      "kind": "CELL",
      "characters": 13,
      "text": "Rhizopus spp."
    }
  ],
